cts, K036 voluntarily withdrew from the trial before the start of the second cycle; therefore, only the first-cycle data were included in PKCS, PKPS, and BES. The Fed test screened 96 subjects; 32 were enrolled, 29 completed the trial, and 3 withdrew early. Subject C001 was withdrawn during the second treatment cycle due to elevated blood HCG levels. Subject C031 was discontinued from the second cycle due to an adverse event (abdominal pain and diarrhea). Subject C030 was withdrawn from the study due to a family member’s hospitalization, this occurred following blood collection conducted 12.0 hours after completion of dosing in the second cycle. FAS, SS, PKCS, PKPS, and BES all had thirty-two subjects. For subjects C001 and C031, only first-cycle data were included in PKCS, PKPS, and BES. Subject C030, who had two cycles of data, was included in PKCS, PKPS, and BES; however, only C max from the second cycle was incorporated into PKPS and BES. As shown in Figure 2 . Figure 2 Subjects flow chart. Flow chart of the subjects in the fasted state ( A ). Drug T is given in cycle 1, Drug R is given in cycle 2 (I). Drug R is given in cycle 1, Drug T is given in cycle 2 (II). Flow chart of the subjects in the fed state ( B ). Drug T is given in cycle 1, Drug R is given in cycle 2 (III). Drug R is given in cycle 1, Drug T is given in cycle 2 (IV). Abbreviation : N, the number of subjects. Plasma Concentration-Time Profile Mean blood concentration-time curves of oral palbociclib were compared between fasting and postprandial conditions in healthy subjects. The results for the test and reference formulations are presented in Figure 3 . Figure 3 Mean plasma concentration-time curves after taking the test (T) and reference (R) preparations in healthy subjects. ( A ) Fasting ( B ) Fed. Pharmacokinetic Metrics Following oral dosing in healthy volunteers (fasting and fed states), the key pharmacokinetic parameters of the test and reference palbociclib tablet formulations are detailed